esp32s31: make the microphone capture, by clocking the receiver and fixing its DMA

Capture ran end to end for the first time with this. Four separate faults, each
of which alone was enough to produce silence, and all four were hidden behind
the position bug fixed in patch 0010 - with the position pinned at the end of
the buffer, ALSA served stale ring contents and every recording came back
byte-identical no matter what the hardware did.

**SIG_LOOPBACK was the wrong mechanism entirely.** It is a *digital* loopback
that feeds the transmitter's data back into the receiver, so it can never carry
the microphone. The driver's own note recorded the giveaway without drawing the
conclusion: "start playback and the ring fills immediately" - that was the
playback data going round the loop. What the receiver actually needs is the
transmitter's *clocks*.

**The receiver was a master with its clocks wired to nothing.** Only the
transmitter's BCK and WS (matrix signals 25 and 27) reach the pads, GPIO3 and
GPIO4. The receiver listens on its own indices, I_BCK 29 and I_WS 30. It now
runs as a slave (RX_CONF bit 3) and the DTS routes 29 and 30 back in from the
pads the transmitter drives.

**The pad input buffers were off.** Routing a matrix input to a pad that is
driven as an output reads nothing unless FUN_IE is set - the same trap the
GPIO7 amplifier hog fell into. Without `input-enable` the stream starts, no
sample ever arrives, and the read fails with EIO after ALSA's wait times out.

**The transmitter stopped itself.** Running for its clocks alone, with no DMA
behind it, TX_STOP_EN - left set by the last playback - halted it at the first
end-of-frame. Measured directly: TX_CONF read 0xc0f214 at trigger and 0xc0f210
a moment later, clocks dead.

Two DMA defects came out of the same investigation. Receive descriptors were
given a length equal to their size, but on receive that field is the hardware's
report of what it stored - declaring it full retires the whole ring at once.
And `rx_node` was never reset when a cyclic descriptor was installed, though
the transmit path resets `tx_node`, so each stream inherited the previous one's
index and reported most of a buffer before a sample arrived.

**Status.** Recordings are full length, error free, and now differ run to run -
RMS 667-730 out of 32768, where before every capture was byte-identical. The
analog front end is live. What is *not* established is the microphone gain: the
vendor's factory_demo sets 30 dB through PGA registers 0x72/0x73 and our driver
exposes only the input-select bits, so those sit at their default. Playing noise
through the speaker did not move the captured level beyond the run-to-run spread
(quiet 667/730/728, noise 733/695/726), but the speaker is itself still faulty,
so that test proves nothing either way.

--- a/drivers/dma/esp32s31-ahb-gdma.c
+++ b/drivers/dma/esp32s31-ahb-gdma.c
@@ -430,10 +430,13 @@
 			size_t chunk = min_t(size_t, remaining, AHB_DESC_MAX);
 			dma_addr_t next = j + 1 == count ? 0 :
 				pool_dma + (j + 1) * AHB_DESC_SIZE;
+			/* Receive reports its own length - see prep_dma_cyclic. */
 			u32 control = FIELD_PREP(AHB_DESC_BUF_SIZE, chunk) |
-				FIELD_PREP(AHB_DESC_DATA_LEN, chunk) |
 				AHB_DESC_OWNER;
 
+			if (direction != DMA_DEV_TO_MEM)
+				control |= FIELD_PREP(AHB_DESC_DATA_LEN, chunk);
+
 			if (j + 1 == count)
 				control |= AHB_DESC_EOF;
 			writel(control, hw);
@@ -526,10 +529,20 @@
 			size_t chunk = min_t(size_t, remaining, chunk_len);
 			dma_addr_t next = pool_dma +
 				((i + 1) % count) * AHB_DESC_SIZE;
+			/*
+			 * On a receive descriptor the length field is the
+			 * hardware's report of how much it stored, so software
+			 * leaves it zero; setting it to the buffer size says
+			 * the buffer is already full and the engine retires
+			 * the whole ring at once without ever waiting for the
+			 * peripheral.
+			 */
 			u32 control = FIELD_PREP(AHB_DESC_BUF_SIZE, chunk) |
-				      FIELD_PREP(AHB_DESC_DATA_LEN, chunk) |
 				      AHB_DESC_OWNER;
 
+			if (direction != DMA_DEV_TO_MEM)
+				control |= FIELD_PREP(AHB_DESC_DATA_LEN, chunk);
+
 			/* EOF closes the period, so one interrupt per period. */
 			if (direction == DMA_MEM_TO_DEV && chunk == remaining)
 				control |= AHB_DESC_EOF;
@@ -822,6 +835,13 @@
 				break;
 			chan->cyclic_rx = desc;
 			chan->cyclic_generation++;
+			/*
+			 * A fresh ring starts at its head.  Without this the
+			 * node index survives from the previous stream, so the
+			 * first position reported is most of a buffer and the
+			 * PCM layer calls an overrun before a sample arrives.
+			 */
+			chan->rx_node = 0;
 			list_del(&vd->node);
 			esp32s31_ahb_start_cyclic(chan);
 			break;
